{"id": "q0353", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "aggressive", "tier": "aggressive", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 1296477934, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "c1188d4ab398808355719c420f025bd920b27aba798f5596eb328457a751cc63", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.5533501505851746, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 3, "query": "Who were the Greek equivalents of the Norns of Norse mythology?", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0353.wav", "input_sha256": "a259133b42ccc87dae273189049c83a9adc1492981527e70fe2cfa2dd921d2b8", "input_samples": 62400, "input_s": 3.9, "n_real_chunks": 4, "score": 0.4725, "act": 0.9999, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "The Greek equivalent s of the N orns are actually the Mo irai. Now, in both cases, we' re talking about these three goddesses who control fate. And so the Norns would be responsible for determining your destiny at birth and then kind of fulfill ing it over time. The Moirai were also responsible for weaving the thread of life, cutting it short when you die, but they were often associated with the Fates as well, which is why sometimes they're called the Three Fates.And I think that's where this idea comes from. So, yeah, there are definitely some parallel s between them.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 30, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0353_aggressive_local.wav", "sha256": "af9f19fd10b2bc7d6d023363f86e005bebe2f40fa987251f1bffba3ce915810a", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 29.76}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.07, 1.127, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.073, 2.13, 1, 0, 0],